theory M6_IpcHandshake
/*
  Model 6 - the real IPC handshake after #149.

  This is the same skeleton as the pre-#149 M6, updated for the two changes
  landed together as the D-4 follow-up:

    * no capability file (issue #149, option B) - the desktop no longer writes
      a per-session bearer to ipc_auth.json (0600). The old Desktop_Channel
      rule published Out(~cap) for the Dolev-Yao adversary; that file is now
      deleted on startup (see ipc.rs IpcServer::start). The endpoint is a
      well-known per-user name (Unix: $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/vela-<uid>/desktop.sock
      with dir 0700; Windows: \\.\pipe\com.vela.VELA.native.<user>) and is
      public knowledge - modelled as a persistent !Listening fact that the
      adversary knows - but knowing it buys nothing. What decides whether a
      connection is accepted is the kernel's answer about who is on the other
      end (ipc_peer + ipc_gate): the peer must be same-UID, be the
      vela-native-messaging-host binary (the compiled Rust host in
      desktopVELA/vela-nm-host), and have a browser among its recent
      ancestors. The transparent stdio relay itself remains subsumed by the
      adversary - only its spawn provenance matters.

    * blast-radius limits (issue #149, option D) - one unlock releases at most
      25 distinct domains, every actual release writes an encrypted audit entry
      naming caller+domain and raises an on-screen toast, and a missed match
      releases nothing. These are quantitative, per-unlock bounds; this
      symbolic model does not count to 25, it proves the underlying
      agreement/injectivity/cross-origin properties that the cap, audit and
      toast make durable. The cap lives above this handshake: it bounds how
      many distinct successful handshakes one unlock is worth, not whether a
      single handshake leaks. See SECURITY_AUDIT.md D-4 "bounded as well as
      stated".

  The rest is unchanged from the previous M6:

    * first-use pairing              - the joining client generates its own
      keypair, computes the fingerprint phrase h(pk) locally, and displays it
      on its own screen. The human compares that phrase out of band and
      confirms; the desktop enrolls a public key only after that human action
      (mirrors enrollment v3: "the joining device computes and displays the
      fingerprint of its own key ... never a value that arrived over the wire").

    * per-client key exchange        - after pairing, each client holds the
      private half of its enrolled key and signs every fill request with it. The
      desktop releases only against a signature verifying under the enrolled
      public key, so request content is bound to a specific client.

    * Approved-grant lifecycle       - issue (human approves exactly one
      (client, origin, item), bound, not a boolean); use (a release consumes the
      grant AND a client-signed request AND a browser-spawned channel);
      anomaly-reconfirm (grants are single use and bound to the triple, so any
      use outside it needs a fresh human issue - encoded as the linear Grant
      consumed by Release); expire and revoke (linear grant removed by an
      explicit rule, so a later use is impossible).

    * request replay cache          - the desktop honours each request nonce at
      most once (`RequestNonceIsFresh`). Without it the agreement below is only
      NON-injective: an earlier version of this theory elided the counter, and
      one captured client-signed request could then be replayed to back several
      different releases (falsified in 13 steps, WITHOUT any client compromise).
      The grant lifecycle still bounded the damage - each release needs its own
      human grant - but the per-client signature was contributing no freshness,
      so calling the result "injective agreement" was wrong. The cache is what
      makes the injective claim below actually hold.

  The adversary is the SDC adversary of M1-M5: a co-resident same-UID process
  with full control of In/Out - except it no longer controls the local IPC
  channel directly. It can compute the well-known endpoint name for free, but
  to make the desktop accept a connection it must be a browser-spawned host
  (honest BrowserSpawned) or have compromised a browser (CompromisedBrowser -
  the "hostile extension / injected browser" residual stated in #149). Model 6
  adds beyond M3: (1) release requires a per-client signature, so the grant
  alone leaks nothing; (2) a browser-spawned channel, so the network alone
  leaks nothing; (3) the compromise of a client is an explicit event, so we
  can prove what a compromised browser can and cannot do.

  Expected verdicts (all-traces unless marked):
    client_key_secret_unless_compromised   VERIFIED  - a client key is secret
        until that client is explicitly compromised.
    unused_item_secret                     VERIFIED  - never-released items stay
        secret even under full client compromise (bulk bound survives).
    release_requires_client_request        VERIFIED  - injective agreement: a
        release needs a human-issued grant for the same (client, origin, item)
        AND the client's own signed request CARRYING THE SAME NONCE (or that
        client's compromise).
    request_is_single_use                  VERIFIED  - the other half of
        injectivity: one release per client request, no replay.
    grant_is_single_use                    VERIFIED  - one release per grant.
    no_release_without_issue               VERIFIED  - every release traces to a
        grant issue.
    enrollment_agrees_with_human_confirmation  VERIFIED - enrollment only ever
        binds a key the joining device displayed and the human confirmed.
    no_cross_origin_release                VERIFIED  - an item stored for O is
        never released for O' != O.
    revoke_prevents_use / expire_prevents_use VERIFIED - the lifecycle states
        are real: a revoked or expired grant cannot be used later.
    compromised_client_cannot_act_as_others   VERIFIED - a compromised browser
        gains nothing beyond its own grants: releases for an uncompromised
        client only happen through that client's own signed request.
    no_release_without_browser_channel     VERIFIED  - NEW in this revision: a
        release for C needs a browser-spawned channel for same C (or that
        browser having been compromised); an arbitrary same-UID process cannot
        cause a release by itself.
    used_item_leaks / release_reachable /
    compromised_browser_residual          (exists-trace) VERIFIED - the consume
        residual and the presence residual both exist.
*/
begin

builtins: signing, hashing

// The desktop listens on a well-known per-user endpoint (Unix socket under
// $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/vela-<uid>/desktop.sock with dir 0700, or the Windows
// named pipe). The name is public knowledge - the adversary can derive it
// without reading any file - so the desktop's willingness to listen is a
// persistent public fact. What is NOT public is the ability to be accepted:
// that requires a browser-spawned host (see below). The old capability file
// rule is gone: no Out(~cap) is ever produced.
rule Desktop_Listen:
    [ ]
  --[ DesktopListening() ]->
    [ !Listening() ]

// Honest case: the browser spawns the VELA native messaging host binary over
// stdio. The host binary identity (vela-native-messaging-host) and the
// browser ancestry check (firefox/chrome/... within MAX_ANCESTRY_HOPS) are
// enforced in ipc_gate; symbolically, only this rule and the compromise rule
// below can produce a Spawned fact for a given client. The adversary cannot
// mint Spawned on its own.
rule Browser_Spawn_Host:
    [ !Listening() ]
  --[ BrowserSpawned($C) ]->
    [ Spawned($C) ]

// Compromised case: a browser the adversary controls can be made to spawn the
// host for any client it chooses. This is the "hostile extension / injected
// browser" residual stated honestly in #149: option B raises the bar from
// "any process" to "a store-signed extension or a browser injection", it does
// not remove the extension itself. The only way the adversary comes by a
// Spawned fact for a client without an honest BrowserSpawned is through this
// explicit compromise event (proved by no_release_without_browser_channel
// without the compromise disjunct).
rule Compromised_Browser_Spawn:
    [ !Listening() ]
  --[ CompromisedBrowser($C) ]->
    [ Spawned($C) ]

// The spawned host connects to the desktop's well-known endpoint. One
// connection per request in the implementation (UnixStream::connect per
// framed exchange); modelled as one Talking fact per release, linear so each
// release needs a fresh browser-spawned connection. A long-lived host that
// reuses one connection for many requests is strictly weaker than this (fewer
// gate checks), so proving with per-request checks implies the real system.
rule Host_Connect:
    [ Spawned($C), !Listening() ]
  --[ HostConnected($C) ]->
    [ Talking($C) ]

// The vault: each credential is minted under exactly one origin.
rule Vault_Setup:
    [ Fr(~cred) ]
  --[ Secret(~cred, $O) ]->
    [ !Vault(~cred, $O) ]

// First-use pairing, joining side: the client generates its own keypair and
// displays the fingerprint phrase h(pk) on its own screen (never read from a
// response - the v3 property). The private half never leaves this fact.
rule Client_Join:
    [ Fr(~sk) ]
  --[ Joined($C, pk(~sk)), SecretClientKey($C, ~sk) ]->
    [ !MyKey($C, pk(~sk), ~sk), !OnScreen($C, pk(~sk)),
      Out(<'join', $C, pk(~sk)>) ]

// First-use pairing, human side: the human reads the phrase h(pk) off the
// joining device's screen and confirms it. The adversary cannot forge this
// out-of-band action.
rule Human_Confirm_Pairing:
    [ !OnScreen($C, pkey) ]
  --[ HumanPairConfirmed($C, h(pkey)) ]->
    [ Confirmed($C, pkey) ]

// First-use pairing, desktop side: enroll a public key only after the human
// confirmed it.
rule Desktop_Enroll:
    [ In(<'join', $C, pkey>), Confirmed($C, pkey) ]
  --[ Enrolled($C, pkey) ]->
    [ !ClientKey($C, pkey) ]

// Per-client key exchange in use: the client signs its fill request with its
// own private key. Only the enrolled key verifies at the desktop.
rule Client_Request:
    [ !MyKey($C, pkey, sk), Fr(~r) ]
  --[ ClientRequests($C, $O, ~r) ]->
    [ Out(<'fill', $C, $O, ~r, sign(<'fill', $C, $O, ~r>, sk)>) ]

// ISSUE: the human approves exactly one (client, origin, item). Bound, not a
// boolean: the grant names the item it may release.
rule Human_Approve:
    [ !Vault(cred, $O), Fr(~g) ]
  --[ Issue($C, $O, cred, ~g) ]->
    [ Grant($C, $O, cred, ~g) ]

// USE: a release consumes the grant AND a request signed by the enrolled
// client AND a browser-spawned channel. The signature binds client and
// origin; the grant binds client, origin and item; the channel binds client
// to a browser ancestor; the item is stored under the same origin. The
// plaintext still reaches the domain (Out) - the consume-stage residual of
// M3, carried over. The blast-radius cap (25 distinct domains per unlock)
// and the encrypted audit+toast are not modelled as counting - they bound
// how many distinct successful handshakes one unlock is worth, not whether a
// single handshake leaks (see SECURITY_AUDIT.md D-4 "bounded as well as
// stated").
rule Desktop_Release:
    [ Grant($C, $O, cred, g), !ClientKey($C, pkey), !Vault(cred, $O),
      Talking($C), In(<'fill', $C, $O, r, sig>) ]
  --[ Released($C, $O, cred, g, r), RequestUsed(r),
      Verified(verify(sig, <'fill', $C, $O, r>, pkey)) ]->
    [ Out(cred) ]

// EXPIRE: the grant is removed; nothing can consume it afterwards.
rule Grant_Expire:
    [ Grant($C, $O, cred, g) ]
  --[ Expired($C, $O, cred, g) ]->
    [ ]

// REVOKE: as above (vault lock / user revoke / device revoke).
rule Grant_Revoke:
    [ Grant($C, $O, cred, g) ]
  --[ Revoked($C, $O, cred, g) ]->
    [ ]

// Compromised-browser case: the client's private key leaks to the adversary.
// This is the ONLY way the adversary comes by a client key (proved by
// client_key_secret_unless_compromised).
rule Corrupt_Client:
    [ !MyKey($C, pkey, sk) ]
  --[ Compromised($C) ]->
    [ Out(sk) ]

restriction VerifyTrue:
  "All x #i. Verified(x) @ i ==> x = true"

// The desktop's replay cache: a given request nonce is honoured at most once.
// This is the replay counter the earlier version of this theory abstracted
// away, made explicit - it is what upgrades the agreement below from
// non-injective to injective.
restriction RequestNonceIsFresh:
  "All r #i #j. RequestUsed(r) @ i & RequestUsed(r) @ j ==> #i = #j"

// ---- Secrecy --------------------------------------------------------------

lemma client_key_secret_unless_compromised:
  "All C sk #i #j.
     SecretClientKey(C, sk) @ i & K(sk) @ j
     ==> Ex #h. Compromised(C) @ h & #h < #j"

lemma unused_item_secret:
  "All c O #i.
     Secret(c, O) @ i
     & not(Ex C g r #j. Released(C, O, c, g, r) @ j)
     ==> not(Ex #k. K(c) @ k)"

// ---- Injective agreement ---------------------------------------------------

// Agreement: every release is backed by a human grant for that exact
// (client, origin, item) AND by the client's own signed request carrying the
// SAME nonce r - or, in the only escape, that client having been compromised.
lemma release_requires_client_request:
  "All C O c g r #i.
     Released(C, O, c, g, r) @ i
     ==> ( Ex #j. Issue(C, O, c, g) @ j & #j < #i )
       & ( (Ex #h. ClientRequests(C, O, r) @ h & #h < #i)
           | (Ex #h. Compromised(C) @ h & #h < #i) )"

// Injectivity, request side: one release per client request. Together with the
// lemma above this is injective agreement in Lowe's sense - the signature is
// carrying freshness, not just authenticity.
lemma request_is_single_use:
  "All C1 O1 c1 g1 C2 O2 c2 g2 r #i #j.
     Released(C1, O1, c1, g1, r) @ i & Released(C2, O2, c2, g2, r) @ j
     ==> #i = #j"

// Injectivity, grant side: one release per human grant.
lemma grant_is_single_use:
  "All C O c g r1 r2 #i #j.
     Released(C, O, c, g, r1) @ i & Released(C, O, c, g, r2) @ j
     ==> #i = #j"

lemma no_release_without_issue:
  "All C O c g r #i.
     Released(C, O, c, g, r) @ i
     ==> Ex #j. Issue(C, O, c, g) @ j & #j < #i"

lemma enrollment_agrees_with_human_confirmation:
  "All C pk #i.
     Enrolled(C, pk) @ i
     ==> Ex #j #h.
            Joined(C, pk) @ j & HumanPairConfirmed(C, h(pk)) @ h
            & #j < #i & #h < #i"

lemma no_cross_origin_release:
  "All c O O2 C g r #i #j.
     Secret(c, O) @ i & Released(C, O2, c, g, r) @ j
     ==> O = O2"

// ---- Grant lifecycle --------------------------------------------------------

lemma revoke_prevents_use:
  "All C O c g #i.
     Revoked(C, O, c, g) @ i
     ==> not(Ex r #j. Released(C, O, c, g, r) @ j & #i < #j)"

lemma expire_prevents_use:
  "All C O c g #i.
     Expired(C, O, c, g) @ i
     ==> not(Ex r #j. Released(C, O, c, g, r) @ j & #i < #j)"

// ---- Browser channel (new in #149) ----------------------------------------

// No release without a browser-spawned channel for the same client, unless
// that client's browser was compromised - an arbitrary same-UID process
// cannot cause a release by itself.
lemma no_release_without_browser_channel:
  "All C O c g r #i.
     Released(C, O, c, g, r) @ i
     ==> (Ex #j. BrowserSpawned(C) @ j & #j < #i)
       | (Ex #j. CompromisedBrowser(C) @ j & #j < #i)"

lemma host_connected_requires_browser_spawn:
  "All C #i.
     HostConnected(C) @ i
     ==> (Ex #j. BrowserSpawned(C) @ j & #j < #i)
       | (Ex #j. CompromisedBrowser(C) @ j & #j < #i)"

// ---- Compromised browser -----------------------------------------------------

// A compromised C2 buys nothing outside its own grants: a release for a
// different, uncompromised C still requires C's OWN signed request, with the
// same nonce the release consumed.
lemma compromised_client_cannot_act_as_others:
  "All C C2 O c g r #i #k.
     Compromised(C2) @ i
     & Released(C, O, c, g, r) @ k
     & not(C = C2)
     & not(Ex #h. Compromised(C) @ h & #h < #k)
     ==> Ex #j. ClientRequests(C, O, r) @ j & #j < #k"

// A compromised browser buys nothing outside its own spawning: a release for
// a different, uncompromised browser's client still requires that client's
// browser to have spawned. This is the dual of the previous lemma for the
// channel.
lemma compromised_browser_cannot_act_as_others:
  "All C C2 O c g r #i #k.
     CompromisedBrowser(C2) @ i
     & Released(C, O, c, g, r) @ k
     & not(C = C2)
     & not(Ex #h. CompromisedBrowser(C) @ h & #h < #k)
     ==> Ex #j. BrowserSpawned(C) @ j & #j < #k"

// ---- Existence ---------------------------------------------------------------

lemma release_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex C O c g r #i. Released(C, O, c, g, r) @ i"

lemma used_item_leaks:
  exists-trace
  "Ex C O c g r #i #j. Released(C, O, c, g, r) @ i & K(c) @ j"

lemma compromised_browser_residual:
  exists-trace
  "Ex C O c g r #i #j #k.
     Compromised(C) @ i & Released(C, O, c, g, r) @ j & K(c) @ k"

end
